BUILD-DISCIPLINE candidates -- THREE patterns from the 2026-07-29 office/nomac session, filed as candidates NOT applied (doctrine files untouched this session; realisateur was BUSY under a foreign interactive session at close, so this comes through the front door). (1) ONE SOURCE, AND PROVE THE READ TOOK. The existing 'config read from one source, not retyped per file' row assumes a single source is sufficient. It is not, if the READER can silently drop the assignment: office.conf declared GROUPS="staff commissio payroll worm", GROUPS is a bash special readonly array (the caller gid list), so sourcing it assigned NOTHING, raised nothing under set -euo pipefail, $GROUPS expanded to 1000, and bootstrap created a mailbox named after a group id while printing success. Mechanized: bootstrap.sh now probes every key in its config for that collision class (bash -c "KEY=__probe__; printf %s \${KEY}") and fails loud. Candidate row: a config key is not read until something proves the value arrived. (2) A DEPLOY SCRIPT'S VALUE IS THAT THE SECOND HOST DISAGREES WITH THE FIRST. Not saving keystrokes. The office ran fine on dexter; landing it on nomac in one scripted command surfaced three defects in ~20 minutes that one host had hidden indefinitely -- systemd --user cannot use ProtectKernelTunables/ProtectKernelModules/ProtectControlGroups (218/CAPABILITIES, tolerated by dexter's newer systemd, refused by 24.04's), a preflight comparing version strings as floats so 3.12 read as older than 3.9, and node/claude miscategorised as fatal when they are the employees' runtime rather than the office's. This is the strongest available argument against the ecosystem's recurring hand-copy-deploy weak spot, and it is a POSITIVE argument (find bugs) not a hygiene scold. (3) THE CHEAP CHECK AND THE REAL CHECK DISAGREE, AND THE CHEAP ONE REPORTS SUCCESS FIRST. A readiness waiter polling TCP 2224 fired 20 SECONDS into a five-minute VM install, because subiquity's own INSTALLER sshd answers on the forwarded port -- port-open was evidence the installer was running and would have been read as the host being ready. Real condition: key-auth login returning hostname==nomac. Sibling of the already-filed 'absence is not the strong claim' candidate: presence of a listener is not presence of the service. Corollary worth keeping: what actually showed the install progressing was a console SCREENSHOT read as an image (VBoxManage controlvm nomac screenshotpng) -- a human-sense witness where no exit code existed.
